    {
      "source_id": "q-102054",
      "db_id": 17592186490239,
      "prompt": "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x?$",
      "solution": "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c632cc2770958b5d73f6e143de0b72d19b2099f9f656ff34f273d88acc4f9cb4.png)",
      "difficulty": {
        ":db/id": 17592186045485,
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
      },
      "profile": "q-102054",
      "choices": [
