    {
      "source_id": "q-109055",
      "db_id": 17592186490285,
      "prompt": "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$",
      "solution": "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=8.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(8) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(8,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c562212687c848e2de25b757f59acd70467a085ae2e0713fbeafa355ad5bb5.png)",
